-- title:
--   Diagonal left translation conjugates the associated order
-- statement:
--   Let $D$ be a ring equipped with a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra structure, let $\Lambda$ be a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule of $D$, let $\gamma$ be a unit of $D$, let $\beta$ be a unit of $D \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}^{f}$, where $\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}^{f}$ denotes the finite adele ring of $\mathbb{Q}$ relative to $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$, and let $z \in D$. Here [`Submodule.finiteAdeleBox`](def/Submodule_FiniteAdeleBox.html#L14) $\Lambda$ is the additive subgroup of $D \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}^{f}$ generated by the tensors $w \otimes a$ with $w \in \Lambda$ and $a$ a finite adele integral at every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$, and for a unit $g$ of $D \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}^{f}$ the $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule [`Submodule.conjByFiniteIdele`](def/Submodule_FiniteAdeleBox.html#L31) $\Lambda\, g$ of $D$ consists of those $x \in D$ for which $x \otimes 1$ lies in the image of that additive subgroup under $u \mapsto g u g^{-1}$; finally [`Submodule.finiteIdeleDiagonal`](def/Submodule_FiniteAdeleBox.html#L43) sends $\gamma$ to the unit $\gamma \otimes 1$. The assertion is the equivalence: $z$ lies in [`Submodule.conjByFiniteIdele`](def/Submodule_FiniteAdeleBox.html#L31) $\Lambda\,((\gamma \otimes 1)\beta)$ if and only if $\gamma^{-1} z \gamma$ lies in [`Submodule.conjByFiniteIdele`](def/Submodule_FiniteAdeleBox.html#L31) $\Lambda\, \beta$. No hypothesis is imposed on $\Lambda$ beyond being a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule, and the conclusion is the element-wise equivalence rather than the submodule identity $\Lambda^{\gamma\beta} = \gamma \Lambda^{\beta} \gamma^{-1}$ that it encodes.
--
--   This is the standard fact that left multiplication of a finite idele by a global unit replaces the associated order $\Lambda^{\beta} = D \cap \beta \widehat{\Lambda} \beta^{-1}$ by its conjugate $\gamma \Lambda^{\beta} \gamma^{-1}$, so that all orders attached to a single double coset $D^{\times} \beta \widehat{\Lambda}^{\times}$ are conjugate. It is used in the Čerednik–Drinfeld coset-graph material, where vertex and edge sets of quotient graphs are matched with class sets of Eichler orders and Hecke arrow degrees are computed via relative indices of conjugated subgroups.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Submodule_mem_conjByFiniteIdele_diagonal_mul_iff.lean

